counterexample:
-- Under the current schema no valid counterexample exists. 
-- (Any data inserted must obey the PK and FK requirements, and under these conditions the two queries always return the same count.)

-- For illustration, here is an example of valid test data where both queries yield the same result:

INSERT INTO table4 (c7, c6) VALUES ('A', '+');
INSERT INTO table4 (c7, c6) VALUES ('B', '-');

INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c7) VALUES ('row1', 'A');
INSERT INTO table1 (c1, c7) VALUES ('row2', 'B');

-- Q1 will join only the row with c7 = 'A' (since only that row in table4 has c6 = '+'), and Q2 will count only 'row1'.
-- Thus, both queries return 1. 
-- In summary, no counterexample (i.e. test data making Q1 and Q2 differ) can be produced without violating the schema constraints.

