counterexample:
-- No INSERT statements can be provided that yield a counterexample without violating the schema constraints.
-- Under the current schema, every row in table5 with c8 = '201309' is unique per c7, so the aggregation in Q1 never combines multiple rows and both queries are equivalent.

-- For example, the following data illustrates the (equivalent) behavior:

INSERT INTO table1 (c7) VALUES (1);
INSERT INTO table1 (c7) VALUES (2);

INSERT INTO table5 (c7, c8, c4) VALUES (1, '201309', 30);
INSERT INTO table5 (c7, c8, c4) VALUES (2, '201309', 20);

-- Q1 will perform the join, group by c7 and compute SUM(c4) (which is 30 for c7 = 1 and 20 for c7 = 2), then order descending and return 1.
-- Q2, filtering on c8 = '201309' and ordering by c4 desc, will also return the row with c7 = 1 (since 30 > 20).
-- Therefore, under the given schema, no counterexample (that is, test data where Q1 and Q2 differ) exists.

